## Why This Is Asked
Tests for payment APIs must guard against duplicate charges and race conditions; idempotency-key patterns are common in real banks and wallets.

## Key Concepts
- Idempotency; retries without duplicates
- Concurrency and race conditions; race-free guarantees
- Input validation, headers, and error propagation
- Tooling and test design for APIs

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example test snippet (pseudo)
async function testIdempotent() { 
  const key = 'abc123';
  let r1 = await post('/payments/process', {amount:100,currency:'USD'}, {'Idempotency-Key': key});
  let r2 = await post('/payments/process', {amount:100,currency:'USD'}, {'Idempotency-Key': key});
  console.assert(r1.transaction_id === r2.transaction_id);
  console.assert(r1.charge_id !== null);
  console.assert(r2.charge_id === r1.charge_id);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this plan for distributed systems with multiple gateways?
- How would you monitor and alert if idempotency fails in prod?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to validate real-time streaming APIs under multi-tenant constraints, including data isolation, ordering guarantees, and resilience to failures. It also covers dynamic feature flags via an admin API, which is common in production environments.

## Key Concepts

- WebSocket streaming and per-tenant scoping
- Message ordering and deduplication in a high-throughput channel
- Reconnection/backoff logic and backpressure handling
- Dynamic feature flags via admin API and their impact on clients
- Fault injection: broker outages, network partitions, and fallback behavior

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal WebSocket test scaffold (conceptual)
const WebSocket = require('ws');
const ws = new WebSocket(`wss://api.example.com/ws/tenantA/orders`);
let lastTs = 0;
ws.on('message', (data) => {
  const msg = JSON.parse(data);
  // verify monotonic timestamp and non-duplication
  if (msg.ts <= lastTs) throw new Error('Out-of-order or duplicate message');
  lastTs = msg.ts;
});
```

